WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 197: George W. Bush: The First Pitch, PEPFAR, and Crawford Ranch (2001–2009)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.440
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.640
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:09.280
[Rebecca] Today we conclude our journey with George W.

4
00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:10.080
[Rebecca] Bush.

5
00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.200
[Rebecca] In the shadow of war and foreign entanglements, Bush possessed a remarkable human touch.

6
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:22.320
[Rebecca] Throwing a strike at Yankee Stadium, saving millions from disease in Africa, and riding mountain bikes in the Texas brush.

7
00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:29.600
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of the first pitch, the miracle of PPFAR, and life at Prairie Chapel Ranch.

8
00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:40.240
[George] On the evening of October 30, 2001, seven weeks after the World Trade Center towers fell, President George W.

9
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:46.880
[George] Bush traveled to the Bronx for Game 3 of the World Series between the New York Yankees and Arizona Diamondbacks.

10
00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.040
[George] Security was unprecedented.

11
00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:59.040
[George] Snipers lined the stadium roof, fighter jets circled overhead, and Bush wore a heavy Kevlar bulletproof vest beneath a navy blue FDNY fleece jacket.

12
00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:05.440
[George] Warming up underneath the stadium, Yankees star shortstop Derek Jetter walked over and asked, Mr.

13
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.840
[George] President, are you going to throw from the mound or the front of the mound?

14
00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:13.360
[George] Bush replied that he planned to throw from the front of the rubber.

15
00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:17.760
[George] Jedder looked at him and said with a grin, you've got to throw from the mound.

16
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:19.760
[George] This is Yankee Stadium.

17
00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.320
[George] And don't bounce it, they'll boo you.

18
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:28.800
[George] Walking out alone onto the diamond, Bush faced 57,000 roaring fans chanting USA.

19
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:39.760
[George] Reaching the top of the rubber, Bush stood tall, nodded to the catcher, wound up, and fired a perfect high-speed strike right down the heart of the plate into Todd Green's glove.

20
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.560
[George] The crowd erupted into an earth-shaking roar.

21
00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:50.480
[George] In that singular pitch, Bush demonstrated to the entire world that America was unafraid, resolute, and standing tall.

22
00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:59.920
[George] Firing that strike right down the heart of the plate at Yankee Stadium provided a powerful emotional catharsis for New York and the entire United States after the attack.

23
00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:09.560
[George] That perfect strike in the Bronx showed the world that American spirit, culture, and love of the national pastime could not be conquered by fear.

24
00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:18.040
[George] That first pitch at Yankee Stadium was an unforgettable demonstration of physical courage and national pride that lifted the spirits of millions.

25
00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:22.520
[George] Whenever the pressures of Washington became unbearable, George W.

26
00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:32.120
[George] Bush retreated to Prairie Chapel Ranch, a 1600-acre cattle ranch near Crawford, Texas, affectionately dubbed the Western White House.

27
00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:38.680
[George] Unlike Eastern presidents who spent weekends at exclusive country clubs, Bush loved raw physical labor.

28
00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:51.560
[George] He spent blistering hundred-degree afternoons wearing work gloves and jeans, cutting cedar brush with a chainsaw and driving foreign dignitaries around the dusty property in his white Ford F 250 pickup truck.

29
00:02:51.560 --> 00:03:06.280
[George] Visiting world leaders like Vladimir Putin, British Prime Minister Tony Blair, and Japanese Prime Minister Junikiro Koizumi were treated to Texas barbecue, country and western music, and white knuckle rides along the creek beds.

30
00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:22.840
[George] A ferocious athlete, Bush replaced jogging with aggressive, high-speed mountain biking, leading secret service agents and visiting journalists on grueling, twenty-mile rides through the Rocky Canyons, frequently leaving younger riders bloodied and exhausted in his dust.

31
00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:33.880
[George] Life at Prairie Chapel Ranch showcased an unpretentious, athletic president who connected with world leaders over manual labor, barbecue, and rugged outdoor recreation.

32
00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:42.280
[George] The Crawford Ranch was an authentic sanctuary where Bush could escape political pretension and reconnect with the rugged Texas soil he loved.

33
00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:51.440
[George] The Crawford Ranch was an authentic expression of Bush's frontier spirit, welcoming allies and friends with warmth, barbecue and open skies.

34
00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:58.720
[George] No modern president was more affectionately lampooned for his spontaneous mangling of the English language than George W.

35
00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:02.800
[George] Bush, giving birth to the beloved cultural term Bushisms.

36
00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:08.000
[George] Speaking off the cuff at rallies and press conferences, Bush famously declared.

37
00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:22.240
[George] And once attempted to quote an old Tennessee proverb with hilarious results, fool me once, shame on, shame on you.

38
00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:24.880
[George] Fool me, you can't get fooled again.

39
00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:30.960
[George] Comedians on Saturday Night Live, led by Will Farrell, made Bushisms a national staple.

40
00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:36.960
[George] But rather than taking offense, Bush leaned into the humor with complete, unpretentious good nature.

41
00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:45.280
[George] Speaking at the 2001 White House correspondence dinner, Bush quipped, I know that people think I'm dumb, but I'm not.

42
00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:47.040
[George] I'm just from Texas.

43
00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:58.400
[George] In a commencement address at Yale in 2001, Bush looked at the cheering graduates and remarked, To those of you who received honors, awards and distinctions, I say well done.

44
00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:03.680
[George] And to the C students, I say you too, can be President of the United States.

45
00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:13.600
[George] Bush's good humored embrace of his famous verbal slips revealed an authentic leader who never took himself too seriously, disarming critics with self deprecating wit.

46
00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:22.800
[George] His self deprecating humor disarmed the bitter polarization of modern politics, showing a leader who remained humble despite holding immense power.

47
00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:30.920
[George] His colorful wit and self-deprecation disarmed opponents, proving that a leader can wield immense power while remaining thoroughly humble.

48
00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:42.680
[George] While Bush's foreign policy is often remembered primarily through the lens of the Iraq War, historians consider his greatest international legacy to be an astonishing humanitarian triumph.

49
00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:47.080
[George] PEPFAR, the president's emergency plan for AIDS relief.

50
00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:53.560
[George] In 2003, Sub-Saharan Africa was being decimated by the HIV AIDS pandemic.

51
00:05:53.560 --> 00:06:01.800
[George] Thirty million people were infected, an entire generation of parents was dying, and millions of orphaned children were left destitute.

52
00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:08.360
[George] High costs for antiretroviral medications meant an AIDS diagnosis was an automatic death sentence.

53
00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:19.960
[George] In his 2003 State of the Union address, Bush boldly proposed $15 billion for a massive federal initiative to purchase and distribute life-saving medications across Africa.

54
00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:27.400
[George] Over the next two decades, PEPFAR became the most successful global public health initiative in human history.

55
00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:41.880
[George] It funded clinics, trained hundreds of thousands of African healthcare workers, and delivered antiretroviral drugs that saved an estimated 25 million human lives, while preventing millions of babies from being born with HIV.

56
00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:53.160
[George] PEPFAR remains the most noble and effective humanitarian initiative of modern times, an enduring monument to American generosity that saved an entire continent from despair.

57
00:06:53.160 --> 00:07:03.000
[George] By providing antiretroviral drugs to millions across Africa, PEPFAR represented the highest expression of American compassion and Christian charity.

58
00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:11.800
[George] PPFAR's triumph in Africa proved that American leadership is at its finest when bringing health, hope, and life to the poorest of the earth.

59
00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:17.840
[George] Bush's deep love of baseball and simple family joys was on display throughout his presidency.

60
00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:29.840
[George] A former part owner of the Texas Rangers, Bush created the White House T Ball Initiative in 2001, transforming the South Lawn of the Executive Mansion into a regulation T ball diamond.

61
00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:41.360
[George] On sunny spring afternoons, Bush would sit on the grass with Laura, eating popcorn and hot dogs while cheering on local little league teams, with baseball legends like Cal Ripkin J.R.

62
00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:44.640
[George] and Ernie Banks serving as honorary commissioners.

63
00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:52.080
[George] Living in the White House was Barney, a feisty Scottish terrier who became an Internet superstar before the age of YouTube.

64
00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:57.280
[George] The White House communications team attached a small video camera to Barney's collar.

65
00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:10.320
[George] Releasing the annual Barney Cam holiday videos showing the terrier trotting down the hallowed corridors of the West Wing, barking at Secret Service agents, and chasing tennis balls past portraits of George Washington.

66
00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:20.560
[George] The White House T ball games and the antics of Barney the Terrier provided charming, joyful glimpses into a first family that cherished the simple joys of sports and pets.

67
00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:29.440
[George] The T Ball Games on the South Lawn opened the White House to local families, celebrating the enduring democratic warmth of the executive mansion.

68
00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:38.080
[George] The T Ball Games on the South Lawn celebrated the joys of childhood and the timeless American traditions that unite families across the nation.

69
00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:46.720
[George] In the autumn of 2008, Bush's final months in office were engulfed by the worst global economic crisis since the Great Depression.

70
00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:56.640
[George] The collapse of major investment banks like Lehman Brothers and the implosion of the subprime mortgage market threatened to bring about the total freezing of global credit markets.

71
00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:05.720
[George] Though ideologically committed to free market capitalism, Bush recognized that government intervention was necessary to prevent a catastrophic depression.

72
00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:20.360
[George] Working with Treasury Secretary Hank Paulson and Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanck, Bush successfully lobbied Congress to pass the $700 billion Troubled Asset Relief Program TARP, stabilizing the American banking system.

73
00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:36.760
[George] Following the historic election of Barack Obama in November 2008, President Bush and Laura Bush welcomed the Obamas to the White House with extraordinary warmth, executing the most seamless, generous and cooperative presidential transition in modern history.

74
00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:43.720
[George] Ensuring the incoming commander-in-chief was fully briefed on all covert operations and national security threats.

75
00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:54.440
[George] The courageous decision to approve TARP prevented a catastrophic collapse of the global financial system, setting aside political dogma to protect ordinary workers and families.

76
00:09:54.440 --> 00:10:05.000
[George] His swift intervention during the 2008 financial collapse prevented economic ruin, demonstrating the courage to act against ideological orthodoxy in a crisis.

77
00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:14.200
[George] His decisive actions during the 2008 banking panic showed the courage of a leader who prioritized the economic survival of everyday citizens.

78
00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:17.960
[George] Retiring quietly to Dallas, Texas, George W.

79
00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:26.120
[George] Bush deliberately stepped off the public stage, maintaining an unbending rule that former presidents should not criticize their successors.

80
00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:35.000
[George] In retirement, Bush surprised the world by taking up painting, tutored by art professors and inspired by Winston Churchill's love of oils.

81
00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:51.280
[George] He spent thousands of hours in his studio painting Vibrant, moving portraits of wounded American service members from the Iraq and Afghanistan wars, publishing a best-selling art collection titled Portraits of Courage with all royalties donated to veterans nonprofits.

82
00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:55.440
[George] He also painted portraits of immigrant leaders and world statesmen.

83
00:10:55.440 --> 00:11:06.880
[George] His playful friendship with former First Lady Michelle Obama, frequently photographed sharing cough drops and laughter at state ceremonies, became a beloved symbol of bipartisan affection.

84
00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:08.080
[George] George W.

85
00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:18.560
[George] Bush proved that history's verdict is measured not in the fleeting passions of contemporary politics, but in the enduring character, decency, and heart of the man who served.

86
00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:29.920
[George] In retirement, his moving paintings of wounded warriors and his warm friendship with Michelle Obama exemplified the quiet grace and decency of an elder statesman, George W.

87
00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:38.720
[George] Bush retired with the quiet dignity of a leader who made agonizing decisions with an honest heart, leaving his legacy to the judgment of history.

88
00:11:38.720 --> 00:11:48.720
[George] His post-presidential paintings of wounded heroes revealed the deep, tender heart of a commander-in-chief who never forgot the sacrifices of his soldiers.

89
00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:49.920
[Rebecca] George W.

90
00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:59.600
[Rebecca] Bush governed through the most turbulent decade of modern times, leaving a legacy of wartime resolve, profound humanitarian triumphs in Africa, and humble good humor.

91
00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:07.680
[Rebecca] To succeed him in 28, the American electorate made history by electing the first African American president, Barack Hussein Obama.

92
00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:17.760
[Rebecca] Next week, we turn to the historic rise of Barack Obama, the boy from Hawaii, the electric 24 keynote speech that launched his career, and the audacity of hope.

93
00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:21.840
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

